Circular Dependencies

Occurs when

A depends on B
B depends on A

Example


@Service
class A {
   @Autowired B b;
}

@Service
class B {
   @Autowired A a;
}


Solutions

1️⃣ Use Setter Injection

Constructor injection fails because both beans need each other at creation time.
Setter injection creates the beans first, then injects dependencies.

Example:


@Service
class A {
    private B b;

    @Autowired
    public void setB(B b) {
        this.b = b;
    }
}

@Service
class B {
    private A a;

    @Autowired
    public void setA(A a) {
        this.a = a;
    }
}


2️⃣ Use @Lazy

@Lazy delays creation of one bean until it is first used, so the cycle is broken at startup.

Example:


@Service
class A {
    @Autowired
    @Lazy
    private B b;
}

@Service
class B {
    @Autowired
    private A a;
}


3️⃣ Redesign architecture

Best long-term fix: extract shared logic into a third service (C) so A and B both depend on C instead of each other.

Example:


@Service
class C {
    // shared logic used by both A and B
}

@Service
class A {
    @Autowired C c;
}

@Service
class B {
    @Autowired C c;
}
